WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.040
There are lots of good reasons
to try and follow a healthier diet—

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.120
you lose weight,
you feel good,

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.920
but the main reason?

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:13.440
To live a longer, happier,
more productive life.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.520
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.400
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.560
Today we feature some
of the research

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.440
on the amount of influence
genetics on longevity.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:28.472
And we start with a study
of thousands of twin pairs.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.960
It has long been said that
the best hope for a long life

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.320
is to choose your parents
wisely.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.800
After all, doesn't longevity
just run in the family?

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.960
Centenarians are people
who live to be at least 100,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.200
and their siblings
are certainly more likely

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.440
to become centenarians
themselves,

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.200
and the parents of centenarians

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.240
have been found to be
more likely to have lived

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.400
to at least 90 years old.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.560
On the other hand,

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.240
the lifespans of spouses
sometimes correlate as much,

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.880
or even more than,
those of genetic relatives.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:07.720
Your partner may have as much
of an impact as your parent.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.200
After all, genes aren't the only
things that get passed down.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.160
Perhaps Grandma's
healthy recipes

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.640
or even a lifelong
love of running

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.280
runs in the family, too.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:20.200
To tease out
the role of genetics,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.480
researchers turn
to twin studies,

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.760
comparing difference between

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.800
identical twins
and fraternal twins.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.200
For example, imagine
you're trying to determine

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.440
the role of genetics
in cancer--

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:33.800
the role played by genes,

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:37.520
versus other factors
that we may have control over.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.200
Since identical twins share
100 percent of their genes,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.480
whereas, on average, regular
twins only share 50 percent,

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.000
if genes play a large role,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.960
then you'd expect that identical
twins would be more likely

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.440
to share the same fate
than fraternal twins, right?

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.240
If there were no difference
in the chances

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.640
that both sets of twins
got the same disease,

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.840
then it would appear
there's no genetic contribution.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.600
It turns out that
the overwhelming contributor

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.440
to the causation of cancer
is not genetics,

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:09.160
but rather,
what we're exposed to.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.040
Primary genetic factors
may only account

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.880
for 5 to 10 percent
of all cancers.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:18.800
The BRCA genes popularized
by Angelina Jolie, for example,

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.960
may account for as little as
2 percent of breast cancers.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:24.920
If one identical twin
gets breast cancer,

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.600
the likelihood the other
will too is only 13 percent,

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:32.440
despite having
essentially identical DNA.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.600
Now, that's higher
than shared rates

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.440
among nonidentical twin women,

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.440
so there is a genetic component,

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.120
but genes only appear
to make a minor contribution

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:42.760
to cancer risk.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.960
This is consistent with
the rates of common cancers

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.120
profoundly differing by as much
as 200-fold around the world.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.840
What do twin studies have to say

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.720
about the heritability
of lifespan?

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.480
Based on a study
of thousands of twin pairs,

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.800
the heritability of longevity

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:04.000
was 26 percent for men
and 23 percent for women.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.240
Subsequent twin studies have
arrived at a similar estimate:

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.800
approximately 25 percent
of our lifespan is determined

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.760
by our genetic differences,

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:14.840
which means
how we live our lives

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.840
may determine
the bulk of our destiny.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.640
Estimates using other methods

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:22.600
tend to fall in
the 15 to 30 percent range.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:25.640
For example, an analysis
of millions of family trees

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.760
from 86 million public profiles
in an online genealogy database

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.560
led to an estimate
of 16 percent,

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:36.360
though due to so-called
"assortative mating,"

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.480
meaning the fact that
we tend to pair up with mates

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.240
similar to ourselves,
rather than at random,

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.960
that may actually be
an overestimate.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:46.760
Chosen partners often have
similar lifestyles,

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:48.960
so some of that
16 percent estimate

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.200
may have been influenced
by families sharing

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.680
similar diets
and healthy habits,

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:54.560
and not exclusively their genes.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.400
Taking that into account,

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.840
the actual heritability
of lifespan may even be

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.360
well below 10 percent.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.960
To leverage
the lifespan leeway we have

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.560
beyond the relatively small
genetic component,

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.560
we must first understand
the aging pathways

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.120
that account for
the nine hallmarks of aging.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:18.880
The term "anti-aging" has been
much abused in popular culture,

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:19.880
attached to all manner

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.320
of unproven products
and procedures.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.840
The term should probably
be reserved for things

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:28.160
that can delay or reverse aging
through the targeting

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:31.400
of one or more of the
established aging mechanisms.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:34.520
In a landmark paper
cited nearly 7,000 times

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.080
in the biomedical literature,

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:37.680
"The Hallmarks of Aging"

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.080
delineates nine
common denominators

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.800
of the aging process.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:46.600
They are Genomic Instability,
the accumulation of DNA damage.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.320
Telomere Attrition,

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.000
the loss of the protective caps
at the end of our DNA strands.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.120
Epigenetic Alterations,

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.600
changes in the way
our genes are expressed.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.440
Loss of Proteostasis,

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.920
the buildup
of misfolded proteins.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:03.400
Deregulated Nutrient Sensing,

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.840
metabolic alterations
particularly sensitive to diet.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:08.760
Mitochondrial Dysfunction,

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.440
the declining efficiency
of our cellular power plants,

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:16.880
Cellular Senescence,
the arrest of cell replication.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:18.840
Stem Cell Exhaustion,

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:22.320
the loss of the potential
of our tissues to regenerate,

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.160
and Altered
Intercellular Communication,

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:28.715
the rise in
proinflammatory signals.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:32.619
In our next story, we look at how
the so-called Alzheimer's gene APOE

00:05:32.619 --> 00:05:35.921
is the single most important
gene for longevity

00:05:35.921 --> 00:05:40.440
because of its role as a cholesterol
carrier throughout the body.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.600
Though the genetic
contribution to lifespan

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.360
may be relatively minor,

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:45.680
are there specific genes

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:48.280
that have been associated
with longevity?

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:50.520
The leading method
for complex genetic mapping

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:53.680
is called genome-wide
association analysis,

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.200
which is essentially
a massive game of Go Fish,

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:58.560
comparing a million or more
letters of DNA

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.000
between groups
of similar people,

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.640
looking for a match.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:02.680
So for example,

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.720
if you stretch out the DNA
of hundreds of centenarians

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:09.400
and compare those sequences
to that of noncentenarians,

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.640
is there a DNA letter
at a certain position

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:15.000
that centenarians share
disproportionately?

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.320
The problem is
hundreds of centenarians

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:21.240
are all researchers are
typically able to find.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:23.280
Extremely long-lived
individuals,

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:24.480
such as centenarians,

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:26.920
comprise only a tiny fraction
of the population,

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:29.800
on the order of
1 in 10,000 or so.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:31.040
As you can imagine,

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:34.240
the more people you have in
a genome-wide association study,

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:35.880
the more likely
you'll be able to find

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:38.600
a needle in the DNA haystack.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:40.720
With so few people
available to study

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.840
for extreme longevity research,

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:46.520
it becomes much more difficult
to identify trends.

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:48.840
Some researchers have tried
solving this problem

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:52.080
by lowering the age requirement
to 85.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:54.960
Then you can enroll
thousands into your study,

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:56.920
but making it to 90

00:06:56.920 --> 00:06:59.520
is not the same
as making it to 100.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:04.920
In fact, statistically, it's
as hard to get from 90 to 100

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:08.240
as it is to get to 90
in the first place.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:10.880
So, by using
younger age brackets,

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:15.840
we may miss out on discovering
some secret centenarian sauce.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.000
That brings us to the largest
genome-wide association study

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:20.560
of lifespan to date,

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:23.680
based on data
from a million people.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:26.920
How were researchers able
to include so many subjects?

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:28.840
By correlating
the genetic fingerprints

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:30.960
of half a million
middle-aged individuals

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.800
with the ages
of both of their parents,

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:34.880
they were able to find

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:38.040
a dozen DNA regions
linked to lifespan

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:40.880
that appeared to account for
up to five years' difference

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:42.160
between individuals.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:46.200
Twelve DNA markers is actually
a surprisingly low number,

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:48.160
compared to height,
for example,

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:52.440
which is determined by more than
400 different DNA spots--

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:56.160
although, unlike lifespan,
height is highly heritable.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:58.000
A review of all
of the genome-wide

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:00.480
association studies
for longevity put together

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.840
only found one gene confirmed

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:05.200
in multiple independent
meta-analyses:

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:08.800
the "Alzheimer's gene,"
APOE.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:11.880
Beyond just determining
lower or higher dementia risk,

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:14.800
APOE is the single
most important gene

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.000
when it comes to longevity--

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:19.960
though again, that's not
necessarily saying much.

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:24.880
APOE codes for a protein
that is 299 amino acids long

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:27.560
called apolipoprotein E.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.480
Some people have APOE genes
that code for the protein

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:33.960
with the amino acid cysteine
at positions 112 and 158,

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:37.640
known as the APOE2 variant,

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:40.440
while others have
an arginine in those spots--

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:43.360
the APOE4 variant
of the protein,

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:46.080
and APOE3 is the third major
type of variant,

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.000
which has one of each.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.840
Having genes that code
for the APOE4 variant

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:53.200
increases your risk
of cognitive decline,

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:57.840
full-blown Alzheimer's disease,
and premature death.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:00.280
If you have one copy
of the APOE4 gene

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:01.320
from your mother or father,

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:02.960
your odds of becoming
a centenarian

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:04.840
are cut roughly in half,

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:07.480
and if you have APOE4 genes
from both parents,

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:12.200
your centenarian odds drop
by more than 80 percent.

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:16.480
What does this protein do
to have such a powerful impact

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:18.840
on our health and longevity?

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:22.260
APOE is the primary cholesterol
carrier in the brain

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:24.600
and plays a major role
packaging and transporting

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.320
LDL cholesterol
throughout the body.

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:30.280
The LDL cholesterol level
in those with APOE4 genes

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:32.040
averages more than
40 points higher

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:33.960
than those with APOE2 genes,

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:38.040
gunking up the arteries that
feed both the heart and brain.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:41.000
LDL cholesterol is a risk factor
not just for heart disease

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:43.720
but Alzheimer's disease
as well.

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:45.840
Switch people to a diet
lower in animal fat

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:47.080
and cholesterol, though,

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:49.760
and those LDL differences
can disappear,

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:52.880
nearly a 60-point drop in LDL.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:54.200
The difference
in cholesterol level

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:56.040
caused by
the different APOE genes

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.120
can simply disappear

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:01.480
if you eat a diet low enough in
saturated fat and cholesterol.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:05.160
So diet can trump genetics.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:08.280
This may explain
the so-called Nigerian paradox.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:10.600
If you inherit one APOE4 gene,

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:12.580
your risk of getting Alzheimer's
may triple,

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:15.360
and if you get APOE4 genes
from both parents--

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:17.760
which occurs in about one
in fifty people in the US--

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:20.200
you might end up
with nine times the risk.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.000
The highest frequency
of the APOE4 variant

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:24.960
occurs in Nigerians,

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:26.840
but they also have
some of the lowest rates

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:28.560
of Alzheimer's disease.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:29.320
How can that be?

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:33.040
The population with the highest
rate of the "Alzheimer's gene"

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:37.000
has one of the lowest rates
of Alzheimer's disease?

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:38.760
This contradiction
may be explained

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.960
by Nigerians' extremely low
blood-cholesterol levels,

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:43.800
thanks to a diet
low in animal fat,

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:47.440
and consisting mainly
of grains and vegetables.

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:48.440
Humans may have evolved

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:52.040
to maintain an LDL level
of about 25,

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:54.080
but the average
in the Western world

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:55.920
is approximately 120.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:59.360
It's no wonder heart disease
is the leading cause of death

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:00.840
in high-income countries,

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:02.000
and Alzheimer's disease,

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:04.080
according to
the World Health Organization,

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:04.960
is killer #2.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:10.520
Too often, doctors and patients
have a fatalistic approach

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:12.120
to chronic
degenerative diseases,

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:14.440
and Alzheimer's is no exception.

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:16.600
"It's all in your genes,"
they say,

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:19.240
"and what will happen
will happen."

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:22.160
But research shows that although
you might have been dealt

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:24.200
some poor genetic cards,

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:30.000
you may be able
to reshuffle the deck with diet.

